  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 PM EDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\n- Areas of frost tonight\n\nSaturday morning temperatures away from Lake Michigan were right\naround 32 degrees, plus or minus a few degrees locally, with \nareas of frost as expected. A more marginal setup for frost is \nexpected tonight, mostly in southern Michigan. As the surface high\npressure ridge migrates southeast of Michigan tonight, low-level\nsouthwest flow will increase to 15-25 knots. While the surface \nlayer should decouple under strong radiational cooling, the \nincreasing pressure gradient winds may keep some locations stirred\nup enough to prevent widespread frost. Model guidance on \novernight lows tonight remains a few degrees higher compared to \nlast night. The frost advisory for southern Michigan, where winds \nwill be relatively weaker, will be the final one issued from the \nGRR WFO until spring.\n\n- Warming trend with fair weather\n\nThe brief shot of cooler air is already exiting, to be replaced by\na warmer mix of Pacific and Southwest continental air. 925-850 mb\ntemperatures between this morning and Sunday morning will rebound\nabout 10 degrees C and remain that way for several days. After a\nbreezy Sunday, calmer winds are favored much of the coming week \nunder weak upper-level flow and a tendency for subsidence and high\npressure over the region. Normal high temperatures are now around\n60 degrees, and the next several days will feature highs trending\nfrom the lower to the upper 60s. Ensembles are in good agreement \nregarding the forecast temperatures and drier conditions through \nat least midweek.",
